hi  let's talk religion so as some of you  know I'm a student of religions but my  area of expertise is in fact Islam and  Sufism and not what is Sufism you may  ask and that's a very good question I  think when a lot of people think Sufism  they get this image and technically yes  that is Sufism but it's in fact a very  specific ritual by a specific order of  Sufism called the nevla via that's based  in Turkey and Sufism is in fact a much  bigger and more complex than this and is  a much more significant part of Islamic  history than many tend to believe now  many people tend to describe Sufism as  the inner dimension of Islam or Islamic  mysticism or associated with ascetic  individuals living in caves in seclusion  in the wilderness but this is in fact  it's a great oversimplification of the  subject and Sufism is a tradition that  has taken on many many forms and diverse  forms throughout history to explain it  properly we'll have to go way back  all the way back to the very inception  of the Islamic religion and the  immediate aftermath after the death of  the Prophet Muhammad in the years  following his death and asked the  Islamic religion was spreading  throughout the world many people  struggled with how to apply the new  religion and answer questions like what  it is and how it should be lived and in  this early phase a lot of different  traditions emerged one was the legal  tradition on his fik which established  the diverse legal system we know today  as Sharia you know that thing that  everyone's so afraid of and this was a  way to establish the right way to live  as Muslim people and as a society to  other traditions that emerged were  theology or Kalam and also philosophy or  fanciful which was inspired by Greek  philosophy and tried to apply a lot of  those ideas to the written sources of  the Koran and the hadith and lastly  there was Sufism known in Arabic as - so  it's difficult to know exactly when  Sufism appeared but we know that in the  eighth and ninth centuries the word Sufi  was used to describe a certain group of  individuals in and around Baghdad  that lived extremely pious and ascetic  lives and would often wear rich sheep  and and is a simple clothing in fact  many believed that the word Sufi comes  from the Arabic word tzuf which means  wool and referred to the kind of  clothing that they would wear at the  time now it's important to note that  these different traditions were in no  way exclusive being a Sufi did not mean  that you rejected shaadi or wasn't  interested in philosophy but these  tended to blend together and was open  sort of part of the early Islamic  history also Sufism should definitely  not be described as a branch of Islam as  like Sunni or Shia because Sufis  throughout history has belonged to both  of these denominations now these early  ascetic individuals would lay the  groundwork what would become a hugely  diverse and influential tradition within  Islam one of the most famous of these  early Sufis was in fact a woman named  Atta be Allah the Weah or Rabi al bara  and she's often described as a very  beautiful woman who turned her back  completely on the world and spent all  her time in devotion and love for God  now this focus on unconditional love  would later become a staple of Sufism at  least some branches within Sufism and it  is perhaps best expressed in one of her  famous sayings if I adore you out of  fear of hell burn mean he'll if I adore  you out of desire for paradise lock me  out of paradise but if I adore you for  yourself alone do not deny me your  eternal beauty now these are the Sufis  eventually evolved in different  directions and are sometimes divided  into two very general categories which  is sober Sufis and ecstatic Sufis a very  famous of the latter is a man by the  name of Mansur al-hallaj was a very  controversial figure both during his  lifetime but also to some degree still  today in a very popular story it is said  of how Lodge after intense spiritual  practice entered an ecstatic state where  he ran out on the streets of Baghdad  shouting Ana al-haq  which is usually translated to I am the  truth now truth or al haq is in fact one  of the so-called 99 names of God and  thus what collage was essentially saying  is  I am God and this of course shocked the  authorities at the time who executed him  in a rather nasty way for daring to  associate himself with God but according  to many Sufis what he was doing was  actually quite the opposite  what halat had done through his intense  spiritual practice was to completely  annihilate himself and become immersed  in the reality of God when he said on  al-haq or I am the truth he wasn't  associating himself with God but he had  taken the eye out of the picture  completely and there was only God left  this to Sufis was in fact the ultimate  sign of humility and which is quite the  opposite of what the authorities thought  and this would also become a main tenant  of their practice it's kind of similar  to the philosophy that Jim Carrey's  obnoxiously trying to convey in every  single interview nowadays in spite of  its great diversity especially later on  there were a few unifying practices and  teachings that can be said to  characterize Sufism generally one of  these is referred to as jihad al nafs or  struggle against the self now the nafs  is a complex word that is usually  translated to everything from ego to  self or lower self it's the part of us  that we usually identify with the eye or  the me so to speak  according to Sufis the nafs is actually  a veil that separates us from God and  thus it should be tamed and eventually  taken out of the equation completely the  goal here is complete annihilation of  the self or not known as fauna in which  the veil is lifted and reality as it is  is revealed what this actually means  this is a debate that's been discussed  throughout history and there are a lot  of different people who have very  different opinions about it but this  also leads us to another unifying  characteristic of Sufism which is  practice how is this fanaa  achieved the most common practice is  something known as thicker meaning  remembrance you've probably seen videos  of dhikr with added dance music on top  as it often involves very intense bodily  movements the curd is a kind of  meditation where the names of God or or  the first half of the Shahada there is  no god but God is repeated over and over  again for long periods of time there are  kinds of vicar that are very intense and  physical there are  that are very melodic and also those  like that of the naqshbandiya order that  is completely quiet what they all have  in common is the intent which is  remembrance of God and thus also  forgetfulness of the self or the nufs  another famous practice is something  known as Samar which means listening and  these rituals often include music or  recitation of poetry or the Quran and as  kind of a similar function to the Vicker  but here ecstatic states are reached  through listening to these things music  or recitation famous examples of Sam I  include the whirling dervishes of the  novella via that I mentioned in the  beginning and also qawalli music made  famous by people like Nosferatu leak on  a Sufism grew in popularity it started  to eventually become more systematized  it's about its teachings and its  practices around the turn of the first  millennium Sufism can be said to have  taken the form that to some degree it  still holds today and this was mainly  for two reasons one was the intellectual  systematization z' of its teachings and  wider acceptance as a result brought on  by people like abu talib al marcus  abdullah mongol salami and perhaps most  famously abba hamid al-ghazali it's not  really historically correct to say that  there existed a Muslim orthodoxy there  was not position to Sufism but  al-ghazali's certainly was one of the  main factors in ensue films wider  acceptance throughout the Muslim world  in his book the revival of the religious  sciences he said to have successfully  created a synthesis between Sufism Fick  our jurisprudence and column-based  theology and which resulted in it's like  I said the wider acceptance also around  this time the practitioners of Sufism  began to organize themselves into  tarukas or orders from tariqa means way  in basically or path many of these  orders still remain today and can differ  dramatically in terms of practice or  philosophy some of the earliest and most  famous of these include the Cod area  shoddily and Naqshbandi raffia and so on  all these orders usually formed around  the Sheikh or a saint which they call in  Arabic wali or friend of God more  properly translated and the teachings  that he is associated with so the  kadriye was formed around a man named  abdulqadir Gilani and the shoddily  around a bajas on our shoddily for  example and the veneration of these  saints is also a great characteristic of  Sufism generally and they're grave sites  or shrines often become a place of  pilgrimage it is thought that these  Saints possess a kind of divine grace  called Baraka which can be received by  just being close to him while he's still  alive or after he's dead  by his gravesite or shrine as that  shrine still possessed some of that  Baraka the teachings and Baraka of these  saints is also passed down in the line  of succession called silsila which is  why the idea of the Sheikh is so  important in Sufism generally speaking  if you are practicing Sufi you need a  sheikh a spiritual master who will guide  you on the path his legitimacy is based  on the line of succession that usually  goes all the way back to the founder of  the order and eventually also to the  Prophet Muhammad himself there's a whole  lot to talk about when it comes to  Sufism and you know can't just fit into  one episode it's that episode we become  way too long so we're gonna have to  divide this into two episodes so I'm  gonna end this one here and I'll release  the next one next week so I'll see you  then  [Music]

